# Pitch 3 — Buy the firm that does not need its owner (Family B)

**The unit and the file.** One small B2B physical business, bought: a specialist distributor,
a B2B textile-care operation or a specialised-cleaning contractor with two to ten staff, asking
€280k–400k (succession memo), financed with equity plus Qredits or a BMKB-backed bank loan. The
"file" is the deal itself: normalised cash flow, the retained account owner, the working-capital
peg, and the debt-service downside written before price is discussed.

**The rule and the date.** No regulatory date; the clock is demographic. Wholesale has the oldest
owners in the CBS series (15.7% aged 65+, 5,210 people) and 94 live listings (boring-business
memo); 78% of installation-sector respondents are thinking about sale or succession, half within
five years (Techniek Nederland, verified).

**Who buys.** The reader is the buyer; the seller is a retiring owner whose accountant has one or
two listings on Brookz or Bedrijventekoop. Competing buyers are strategic consolidators (in
cleaning) and other individuals; the reader competes on process discipline and on paying for
diligence the others skip.

**Why you.** Capital, the engineering habit applied to quoting, job costing and quality, CEE
sourcing on the supply side, and crews that are often Polish- or Ukrainian-speaking. The Dutch
gate is the customer relationship: the succession memo's rule is that the Dutch account owner
stays through the change of control, on paper, before closing.

**The precedent.** The whole "search fund at small scale" trade; the memos cite Brookz's
barometer multiples (3.6× small EBITDA, 4.6–5.7 in cleaning) rather than a single firm. The
specialised-cleaning example carries its warning: 16.2% margin, wages at 52.6% of revenue,
churn rising (verified, CBS).

**The ninety-day test.** Broker one paid order between a CEE producer and a Dutch buyer through
an existing distributor before bidding on anything: proves the supply edge and puts the reader in
front of sellers as an operator, not a tourist. Under €1,000.

**Steps two and three.** Step two: one acquisition with the seller on an earn-out and the account
owner retained. Step three: a second, adjacent firm on the same back office, or the CEE supply
line turned into the firm's product.

**The AI line.** Distribution, textile care and cleaning are handling, logistics and labour; AI
takes the back office, which is where the reader's engineering habit already sits.

**The kill fact.** Normalised cash flow after replacing the seller's unpaid labour cannot service
the acquisition debt, or a wage rise under the collective agreement lands after closing. The
accountant's normalised figures, checked against payroll, answer it.

**The number to settle first.** Qredits' and the banks' actual acquisition-finance terms for a
first-time buyer with €100k equity (the memo has the ceiling and fee; the conditions are open).

**Verdict under the four assumptions.** Ranked third. Hands-on, but not beside a job: the day the
deal closes the reader runs a company, so this pitch assumes the job ends at step two. Capital is
the whole €100k plus debt. If the ceiling is really €50k, this fails; if the reader wants passive
income, this fails harder, because the memo's first rule is that owner-independent firms are rare.
# Edge inventory — draft for the author to correct (2026-09-03)

What the reader has that a typical Dutch small-business starter does not. Every research brief
under `drafts/research/` screens candidates against this list. Correct it; wrong lines here mislead
every run downstream.

| edge | what it is | what it is worth | what it is not |
|---|---|---|---|
| Languages | English (working), Russian and Polish (native-level or near), Dutch A2 | Customers, suppliers and staff in the Polish, Russian-speaking, Ukrainian and Belarusian communities in NL; Polish manufacturers as a supply side; cross-border B2B in English | Not a Dutch-facing consumer business; not regulated Dutch-language exams |
| Background | Eastern European schooling and network; Belarusian origin; long residence in NL | Trust and reach inside diaspora and CEE trade that a Dutch founder cannot buy | Not a substitute for Dutch licences or local relationships |
| Capital | ~EUR 100k, ceiling for the first test (to be confirmed) | Buys a small business with bank debt, a distribution stock position, one licensed setup | Not enough for housing property or a franchise fee plus fit-out |
| Regulatory literacy | Reads EU and Dutch law and implementation directly; four books of it (CRA, Data Act, DPP, GTO, EUDI, machinery) | Compliance as a moat in distribution or services; sees deadlines before the market | Not legal advice; counsel still needed |
| Engineering habit | Automates, integrates, measures; systems thinking | Operations that others run on spreadsheets and WhatsApp; evidence trails | Must not become an IT business again |
| Research pipeline | The book machine: evidence-led screening, panel review, verdicts | Can screen 10 options for the cost of one consultant's afternoon | Screening is not selling |
| Audience | ~30,000 LinkedIn followers, professional | A channel for B2B offers, recruiting partners, testing demand | Not a consumer channel; not to be turned into a content business |
| Constraints | A2 Dutch; day job (?); family (?); risk appetite (?); retraining tolerance (?) | | The four open constraints: passive vs hands-on; alongside the job or replacing it; capital ceiling; two years of retraining or not |

